<!-- 4. OVERVIEW -->
<section>
    <h3>The Four Pillars</h3>
    <div class="question-box">If you had to teach a computer to solve a real-world problem, what thinking tools do you need?</div>
    <div class="box">
        <p class="fragment"><span class="keyword" data-def="Abstraction: focusing on essential information while ignoring irrelevant detail to create a simplified model.">Abstraction</span> — keep what matters, ignore noise.</p>
        <p class="fragment"><span class="keyword" data-def="Decomposition: breaking a problem into smaller manageable sub-problems before coding.">Decomposition</span> — split the problem into parts.</p>
        <p class="fragment"><span class="keyword" data-def="Pattern recognition: identifying similarities to reuse solutions and build general rules.">Pattern recognition</span> — notice repetition and structure.</p>
        <p class="fragment"><span class="keyword" data-def="Algorithmic thinking: designing a finite, ordered set of unambiguous steps to solve a problem.">Algorithmic thinking</span> — define clear steps with predictable outcomes.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 5. ABSTRACTION -->
<section>
    <h3>1) Abstraction</h3>
    <div class="question-box">Why is a map useful even though it leaves out almost everything?</div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment">Abstraction extracts the essential features for a purpose.</p>
            <p class="fragment">In CS, it can mean hiding implementation details behind functions/classes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-map"></i> TOK link</h4>
            <p class="small-text fragment">“The map is not the territory” — naming and generalising are acts of abstraction.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
    <section data-background="#fff3e0">
        <h3>🧠 Quick Check</h3>
        <div class="question-box">In a weather app, which is a better abstraction: raw sensor voltages or “temperature in °C”?</div>
        <div class="fragment box-blue">
            <p><strong>Temperature in °C</strong> — it’s meaningful and hides irrelevant electrical detail.</p>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

<!-- 6. DECOMPOSITION -->
<section>
    <h3>2) Decomposition</h3>
    <div class="question-box">How do you solve a complex problem without getting lost?</div>
    <div class="box">
        <p class="fragment">Decomposition breaks the <strong>problem</strong> into smaller sub-problems.</p>
        <p class="fragment">It happens <strong>before coding</strong>: what are the parts, inputs, outputs, constraints?</p>
        <p class="fragment">Good decomposition leads to clear modules and responsibilities.</p>
    </div>
    <section data-background="#fff3e0">
        <h3>🧠 Quick Check</h3>
        <div class="question-box">Is “split my program into files” decomposition?</div>
        <div class="fragment box-blue">
            <p><strong>Not necessarily.</strong> Decomposition is breaking down the <em>problem</em> first; files are an implementation choice later.</p>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

<!-- 7. PATTERN RECOGNITION -->
<section>
    <h3>3) Pattern Recognition</h3>
    <div class="question-box">When you’ve solved one problem, how can it help you solve the next?</div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment">Patterns are similarities that allow reuse.</p>
            <p class="fragment">In programming, patterns lead to functions, classes, and libraries.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-repeat"></i> Example</h4>
            <p class="small-text fragment">If you repeat a calculation in many places, that’s a hint to make a function.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 8. ALGORITHMIC THINKING -->
<section>
    <h3>4) Algorithmic Thinking</h3>
    <div class="question-box">What makes a set of instructions a “real” algorithm?</div>
    <div class="box">
        <p class="fragment">An <span class="keyword" data-def="Algorithm: finite, ordered set of unambiguous steps that produces an outcome.">algorithm</span> is finite, ordered, and unambiguous.</p>
        <p class="fragment">It produces a predictable outcome for valid inputs.</p>
        <p class="fragment">Algorithms can be expressed using pseudocode or flowcharts.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="fragment box">
        <p><strong>🔗 Connection to B1.4:</strong> we’ll design and evaluate algorithms systematically.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 9. INDUSTRY APPLICATIONS -->
<section>
    <h3>Where Computational Thinking Shows Up</h3>
    <div class="question-box">If you understand the pillars, what real domains become easier to analyse?</div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Games:</strong> abstraction filters irrelevant detail to focus on mechanics.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Databases:</strong> decomposition shapes tables and relationships.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Security:</strong> decomposition models attack surfaces and defences.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Data analysis:</strong> pattern recognition finds trends.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Machine learning:</strong> pattern recognition at scale (predicting outcomes).</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 10. MISCONCEPTION CHECK -->
<section>
    <section>
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-check-double"></i> Misconception Check</h2>
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>A.</strong> Abstraction means ignoring details that might matter to the goal.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>B.</strong> Decomposition happens after coding, when you refactor.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>C.</strong> Pattern recognition helps reuse solutions and build general rules.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>D.</strong> An algorithm must terminate (be finite).</p>
        </div>
        <p class="small-text" style="text-align:center; margin-top:0.6em;">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-down blue"></i> <span class="blue">Press Down</span> for answers
        </p>
    </section>

    <section data-background="#f4f6f8">
        <h3>Answers</h3>
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>A: False.</strong> Good abstraction keeps the details relevant to the goal and removes the rest.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>B: False.</strong> Decomposition should come before coding.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>C: True.</strong> That’s exactly what it enables.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>D: True.</strong> If it doesn’t terminate, it’s not a valid algorithm for a problem with an endpoint.</p>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

